raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "How does online partisan polarization around lockdowns, masks, and vaccines vary across U.S. states and Canadian provinces?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Our work addresses this need by providing a comprehensive, large-scale study of geographic and event-driven variation in online partisan polarization surrounding COVID-19 discussions across American states and Canadian provinces.", "page": 3},
          {"text": "Our analysis focused on three specific pandemic-related topics: lockdowns, masks, and vaccines.", "page": 4}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "How quickly does polarization change, and do its largest peaks coincide with political events or public health announcements?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Our analysis of daily partisan polarization at the national level (Fig 13) reveals rapid daily fluctuations. This aligns with our\nhypothesis that language adapts quickly in anticipation of or response to specific events", "page": 16}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "How does polarization relate to regional political leanings, conspiracy-related discourse, and subsequent vaccination uptake?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Specifically, we analyze millions of\ntweets to determine how polarization correlates with: (1) the ideological leanings of different regions; (2) exposure to conspiracy theory-related content; and (3) vaccination rates.", "page": 3}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does daily polarization track pandemic severity as measured by reported cases and deaths?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Here, we investigate the aggregated polarization over time for each country and how it relates to the reported number of New Cases and Deaths for\nCOVID-19.", "page": 27}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Can language differences serve as a proxy for polarization without mistaking ordinary differences in group vocabulary for political division?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Therefore, dissimilar language alone does not necessarily indicate polarized\npositions.", "page": 4},
          {"text": "However, when examining discussions on the same contentious topic, language dissimilarity between different partisan\ngroups more likely reflects alternative semantic understandings of that specific topic—a phenomenon we hypothesize\ncorrelates strongly with polarization.", "page": 4}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Partisan polarization in pandemic discourse was not uniform: it varied by region, political leaning, issue, and event, with substantial differences between the United States and Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "polarization is not uniform but varies significantly by geography, political leaning, and public health topic, with substantial\ndifferences between the two countries.", "page": 25},
          {"text": "The temporal patterns\nin our polarization metric successfully captured real-world political events, providing a powerful lens through which to\nunderstand how public discourse evolves in response to political messaging and policy decisions.", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Measuring how differently partisans discuss the same topic provides a scalable way to track online polarization across places, dates, and issues, including outside the pandemic context.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "In this section, we describe our method for classifying users as conservative or liberal and justify\nour choice of topic-conditioned language dissimilarity as a proxy for partisan polarization.", "page": 3},
          {"text": "While our study focuses on the COVID-19 context, the proposed\nmethodology for quantifying partisan polarization at scale can be adapted to examine large-scale discussions across\nvarious temporal, spatial, and topical dimensions.", "page": 3}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors collected a 1% real-time Twitter stream from October 9, 2020, to January 4, 2021. The resulting COVID-related corpus contained about 12.6 million U.S. tweets and 46.6 million Canadian tweets.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We used Twitter’s (X) official API to collect 1% of real-time tweets for the United States and\nCanada from October 9, 2020 to January 4, 2021.", "page": 4},
          {"text": "This process yielded 12,552,213 tweets from 2,657,355 users for the United States and 46,636,206 tweets from\n1,757,675 users for Canada that shared COVID-19-related content.", "page": 5}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The initial filters differed by country: U.S. collection emphasized election terms, whereas Canadian collection included province names and explicit COVID identifiers.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "The U.S. keyword\nlist was dominated by election-related terms, then filtered for COVID-19 terms. By contrast, the Canadian keyword list\nincorporated province names and explicit COVID-related identifiers, which likely offered stronger pandemic relevance.", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Official case and death data came from the U.S. CDC and the Canadian government. Regional vaccination records measured receipt of at least two doses about a year after the Twitter collection.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We used officially reported COVID-19 new cases and death data from each country.\nFor the United States, we sourced data from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). For Canada, we use\nthe data reported by the Canadian government.", "page": 5},
          {"text": "We used officially reported vaccination rates from one year after our Twitter\ndata collection period, as COVID-19 vaccines were only created and approved at the very end of our initial data\ncollection. The vaccination rates represent those who obtained at least two doses.", "page": 5}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Topic labels drew on hashtags screened by two political scientists and supplemented from prior studies. Tweets could receive multiple labels, and the conspiracy category included both promotion and rebuttal.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Two political scientists manually annotated this list with topic and relevance labels, narrowing it to 631 relevant\nhashtags.\n3. We merged these with hashtags identified in previous studies for the same topics [45–47].", "page": 5},
          {"text": "For\nconspiracy-related tweets, relevant indicates that the content pertains to COVID-19 conspiracy theories, including tweets\nthat either promote the conspiracy or debunk / refute it, i.e., any tweet discussing the theory in either context. A tweet\ncould belong to multiple topics.", "page": 5}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors adapted RoBERTa separately for each country and fine-tuned it for topic classification using manually reviewed tweets and lower-weight hashtag labels. They report near-perfect F1 scores on the manually annotated validation data.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We then domain-adapted RoBERTa-base models [41] on the COVID-19 tweets from each country’s dataset through\nself-supervised learning (predicting masked words within tweets), creating country-specific pre-trained language\nmodels.", "page": 5},
          {"text": "During training, we also incorporated hashtag-annotated tweets as weak supervision, assigning them a reduced loss weight of 0.9 compared to 1.0 for\ngold-standard labels.", "page": 5},
          {"text": "Table 1 reports the performance of the fine-tuned RoBERTa classifiers against the manually annotated validation set\n(columns 2–4), showing near-perfect F1-scores across topics and high inter-annotator agreement (Cohen’s Kappa).", "page": 5}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors accepted self-reported locations when Open Street Map and ArcGIS agreed to within one degree. The resulting regional user counts correlated strongly with census populations in both countries.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We processed this free-form text field using Open Street Map and the ArcGIS API, which both return latitude and\nlongitude coordinates when a location is found. We established a clear geolocation when both APIs returned coordinates\nwithin one degree of each other", "page": 6},
          {"text": "In total, we successfully geolocated 757,601 U.S. users with a strong correlation of 0.98 (n = 52, p = 9.27e-35, CI=[0.96,\n0.99]) to official population census data, and 282,454 Canadian users with a strong correlation of 0.92", "page": 6}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Party inference started with politically explicit profile text and then used those labels to train an activity classifier. The activity classifier averaged each user's tweet embeddings and used a multilayer perceptron, with minimum activity thresholds of five tweets in Canada and ten in the U.S.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We first identified politically explicit users based on their profile descriptions, using partisan keywords for each country’s major parties.", "page": 6},
          {"text": "We then used these profile-based classifications as labels to train a classifier based on user activity\npatterns.", "page": 6},
          {"text": "For the activity classifier, we generated user embeddings by aggregating tweet embeddings\nfrom each user (using mean pooling), then trained a multi-layer perceptron to predict party affiliation.", "page": 8},
          {"text": "We filtered users\nbased on their activity level (number of COVID-related tweets), with optimal thresholds determined to be 5 tweets for\nCanada and 10 tweets for the United States.", "page": 8}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The comparison used Democrats and Republicans in the United States and combined five Canadian parties into left and right families.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "For the United States, we identified supporters of the Democratic (left) and Republican (right) parties. For Canada, we\ngrouped users into left (Liberal Party, New Democratic Party, and Green Party) and right (Conservative Party and People’s\nParty) partisan families.", "page": 4}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "External validation compared regional party-label ratios with election returns and checked individual U.S. labels against voter records in five states and Washington, D.C. The regional correlations were about 0.8, and voter-record accuracy was about 73–74%.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "For the U.S. and Canada, we calculated the correlation between the ratio of liberal to\nconservative-labeled users per region in our dataset compared to official election results, obtaining strong correlations of\n0.802 for the United States and 0.815 for Canada", "page": 8},
          {"text": "we matched Twitter users with primary voter registration records available for five states (Ohio, New York,\nFlorida, Arkansas, North Carolina) and Washington DC.", "page": 8},
          {"text": "Our profile classifier achieved 74.35% accuracy and our activity classifier achieved 73.35% accuracy when compared\nto these official records.", "page": 10}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The polarization index averages relevant tweet embeddings into one vector per user and measures how distinctly party groups cluster. Its endpoints are zero for no polarization and one for complete separation.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "To obtain a user embedding for a given topic, we aggregate all\nof the user’s relevant tweet embeddings by averaging.", "page": 10},
          {"text": "We then measure polarization by quantifying how far apart the language of left and right-leaning users is positioned\nin this embedding space, using the C-index [42], a robust clustering measure.", "page": 4},
          {"text": "This index ranges from 0 (no polarization) to 1 (extreme polarization where partisan groups are completely isolated from\neach other in the embedding space).", "page": 10}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "To make the index tractable at scale, the authors repeatedly subsampled users and increased the sample until estimates met a variability threshold. In validation on daily lockdown and vaccine tweets, absolute error fell below 0.001 at roughly 10,000 users.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Our algorithm (Algorithm 1) repeatedly samples a fraction of users, calculates the polarization index on this sample,\nand increases the sample size until the coefficient of variation falls below a specified threshold (e.g., 10 [48]).", "page": 11},
          {"text": "We validated our approximation method on daily lockdown and vaccine tweets. As shown in Fig 4, the absolute error\ndrops dramatically at around 3,000 users and falls below 0.001 at approximately 10,000 users.", "page": 11}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Regional results were based on 12 weeks of weekly polarization scores, and the main vote comparison used the 2020 U.S. presidential election and the 2019 Canadian federal election.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Color intensity from light to dark gives the amount of polarization measured weekly between October 11, 2020 to January 3, 2021 and then averaged\nover the 12 weeks.", "page": 13},
          {"text": "When examined against partisan voting patterns (using the 2020 U.S. presidential election and 2019 Canadian federal\nelection)", "page": 13}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For the national time series, the authors examined both preselected events and events inferred from the highest topic-specific peaks. They also averaged across topics and aligned the resulting scores around events.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "In particular, we examined two types of events: 1) pre-selected political and\nvaccine-related events (Table 8, shown as vertical lines in Fig 13) and 2) highly polarized events detected through analysis of the highest peaks in polarization (Tables 9 and 10, shown as red circles in Fig 13).", "page": 16},
          {"text": "To evaluate the overall responsiveness of our measure to polarizing events, we computed\naverage polarization scores across topics (Fig 14A,B) and performed event-triggered averaging.", "page": 19}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The conspiracy analysis compared daily conspiracy-related posting with aggregate daily polarization. The supplementary pandemic-severity analysis weighted each topic's daily score by its share of COVID-related tweets that day.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Finally, we explored the relationship between conspiracy discourse and polarization using time-resolved measurements\nof conspiracy-related tweets compared with aggregate daily polarization.", "page": 20},
          {"text": "To compute the daily aggregate polarization measure, we employ a weighted sum of each topic’s polarization, considering the percentage of each topic’s tweets within that day’s volume of COVID-19-related tweets.", "page": 27}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Conservative regions tended to be more polarized, but this was not universal. Republican vote share was significantly associated with mask and vaccine polarization, not lockdown polarization. Canada had lower overall polarization and no significant regional vote-share associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We find\nstrong, significant correlations in the US between Republican vote share and polarization around masks and vaccines,\nthough not for lockdowns. For Canada, these associations are not statistically significant, partly due to the smaller number\nof regions analyzed.", "page": 16},
          {"text": "Canada, while showing similar patterns, demonstrated significantly lower overall polarization levels.", "page": 25},
          {"text": "However, this pattern was not\nuniversal. Delaware, a liberal state, exhibited unexpectedly high polarization", "page": 23},
          {"text": "several conservative states like Arkansas, Alabama, and Idaho showed low polarization levels", "page": 23}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "U.S. states with more polarized vaccine discourse had substantially lower subsequent vaccination rates (r = −0.77). Canada did not show the same inverse pattern; a supplementary analysis excluding Nunavut instead found a positive correlation across 12 regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "The correlation is −0.77 with CI = [−0.86, −0.62] (n = 51, p = 6.97e-11).", "page": 18},
          {"text": "We did not observe a similar pattern in Canada, due to small sample size and the implementation of vaccine mandates.", "page": 16},
          {"text": "We remove Nunavut as an outlier because of its very small population. The correlation is 0.74 with CI = [0.31, 0.92] (n = 12, p = 0.004).", "page": 26}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Many large polarization spikes coincided with contentious events, such as a U.S. dispute over mask mandates or a Toronto protest. On average, polarization rose and fell roughly symmetrically over a few days.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "While direct causal evidence is not available, we find that many of the largest polarization peaks coincide with\nhighly contentious events specific to each country’s context.", "page": 17},
          {"text": "Another significant peak occurred\non November 14, 2020, after Biden proposed mandatory mask mandates and South Dakota Governor Kristi Noem\nannounced her opposition.", "page": 18},
          {"text": "In Canada’s lockdown discourse (Fig 13B), the highest peak occurred on October 17, 2020, coinciding with a large\nToronto anti-mask protest against COVID-19 measures.", "page": 18},
          {"text": "This aggregate analysis (Fig\n14c) confirms a rapid, symmetric rise-and-decay profile around polarization peaks, occurring on timescales of days.", "page": 19}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Conservative users posted conspiracy-related content at higher rates in both countries, but daily conspiracy volume had a small negative association with overall polarization only in the United States.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "show that conservative partisans in both countries tweet conspiracy-related content at higher rates than progressive\npartisans.", "page": 21},
          {"text": "When correlated with aggregate daily polarization (Fig 15 B and D, we find a small but significant negative correlation\nfor the United States, while no significant correlation was observed for Canada.", "page": 21}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Daily aggregate polarization was not significantly correlated with pandemic severity in either country.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We\nobserve that polarization is not correlated with the severity of the pandemic, in both the United States and Canada.", "page": 27}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "Semantic similarity is a proxy, not a direct measure of polarized positions; the embeddings may miss relevant signals.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "First, we measured partisan polarization only through the proxy of semantic similarity, which may in certain cases obscure signals not captured by semantic\nembedding representations.", "page": 24}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Averaging tweets by user can conceal tweet-level nuances such as sarcasm and indirect disagreement.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Because our measure aggregates tweets into user-level embeddings, it captures\noverall partisan alignment at the user level, rather than fine-grained nuances in individual tweets. As a result, subtle rhetorical strategies such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement may be less apparent", "page": 10}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Canadian left–right grouping does not distinguish individual parties within each family and excludes Bloc Québécois supporters and other minor-party supporters.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Hence, we grouped the parties based on their partisan leaning.", "page": 8},
          {"text": "We also\nremoved supporters of other minor parties and the Bloc Quebecois.", "page": 8}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Self-reported affiliation can be inaccurate, voter registrations may not reflect current online leanings, and name-based matching can miss people.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Additionally, our classification into partisan groups relies\non self-reported information, which may not be entirely accurate.", "page": 24},
          {"text": "voter registrations may be outdated or not fully reflect\ncurrent partisan leanings expressed online. We note that name-based matching can miss users due to nicknames, misspellings, or common names", "page": 10}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Twitter users form a more politically engaged and often more partisan subset, so the results do not represent the general public in either country.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Third, our analysis is based on Twitter (X) data, which may not fully capture the views and sentiments of the broader\nAmerican and Canadian public. Twitter users are more politically engaged and often more partisan than the general population, which means our results reflect dynamics within a vocal subset of online political discourse.", "page": 24}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The English-only analysis can bias the Canadian results, especially the high polarization estimate for predominantly French-speaking Quebec.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Fourth, we restricted\nour analysis to English-language tweets, which in Canada means we primarily captured perspectives of either anglophones or bilingual francophones — a potential source of bias. For example, the high polarization observed in Quebec\nregarding COVID-19 measures may be influenced by this language limitation.", "page": 24}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Geographic assignments are vulnerable to false, incomplete, or joking profile locations, as well as error introduced by VPNs and fake accounts.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "geographic attribution of users depends on self-reported location fields processed through tools such as Open\nStreet Map.", "page": 24},
          {"text": "it is subject to inaccuracies if users provide false, incomplete, or", "page": 24},
          {"text": "humorous locations. Furthermore, the use of VPNs or fake accounts (including bots) may introduce additional error.", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Automated accounts may inflate measured polarization by contributing disproportionately to partisan content.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Automated accounts can contribute disproportionately to partisan discourse by generating large volumes of ideologically\nslanted content or amplifying existing narratives, potentially inflating the apparent semantic distance between political\ngroups.", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Differences in keyword selection may account for some observed differences between the countries.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "This asymmetry may have contributed to cross-national differences in patterns of observed polarization, and this should\nbe considered when interpreting the comparative results.", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The gap between late-2020 discourse and later vaccine uptake makes their relationship suggestive of possible prediction, not proof that discourse caused vaccination behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Seventh, there are temporal mismatches between our Twitter dataset (collected in late 2020) and regional vaccination rates, which occurred primarily in 2021. As a result, correlations between polarization in vaccine-related discussions\nand later vaccination uptake should be understood as suggestive rather than causative.", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The small number of Canadian jurisdictions limits the regional comparisons, and the authors do not draw a firm conclusion from the positive vaccination correlation in the supplementary analysis.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "For Canada, these associations are not statistically significant, partly due to the smaller number\nof regions analyzed.", "page": 16},
          {"text": "However, with the few points, we do not have\na definite conclusion for this result.", "page": 26}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Timing coincidences do not establish that particular events caused polarization spikes, and the analysis does not isolate a single reason why conservative regions were often more polarized.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "While direct causal evidence is not available, we find that many of the largest polarization peaks coincide with\nhighly contentious events specific to each country’s context.", "page": 17},
          {"text": "While our analysis did not identify a single explanation for the relationship between conservative politics and higher\npolarization, state-specific trajectories provide insights.", "page": 23}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not quantify how much cross-border information flows or social networks shaped polarization and later health outcomes.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Additionally, the role of cross-border\ninformation flows in shaping polarization deserves further investigation, especially as online communities increasingly\ntranscend national boundaries while still being influenced by local political contexts.", "page": 26},
          {"text": "social networks clearly contributed to the diffusion of partisan opinions during the COVID-19 pandemic, but quantifying\ntheir precise impact on polarization and subsequent public health outcomes remains an important challenge for future\nwork.", "page": 26}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend computational work that measures polarization with contextual text embeddings. They treat embeddings as complementary to dictionary methods and choose a text-only approach for accessibility and scale rather than rejecting network methods as ineffective.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Embedding-based models\ncapture latent semantic distinctions between ideological groups, providing methodological advantages that complement\ntraditional lexicon- or dictionary-based strategies.", "page": 3},
          {"text": "Consequently,\nour study adopts a strictly text-based method, which remains both accessible and scalable for analyzing large-scale digital\ndiscourse.", "page": 3}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors build on prior research on partisan divisions over COVID-19 by extending it to geographic differences and event-linked changes over time.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "In line with previous research ((e.g., [17,58–60])), we found that more right leaning states and provinces exhibited\ngreater partisan divisions around COVID-19 on Twitter (X), particularly concerning mask mandates and vaccine\ndistribution.", "page": 22},
          {"text": "However, we extended beyond these studies to characterize the geographic heterogeneity and temporal\nevolution of polarization, connecting features in our polarization metric to real-world events.", "page": 22}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Albrecht reports that vaccination rates were lower where Republican vote share was higher. The authors build on that result by showing that a separate measure, the polarization of vaccine-related language, was also negatively associated with later U.S. vaccination rates.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "This aligns with the well-documented negative correlation between Republican vote\nshare and vaccination rates [50].", "page": 16},
          {"text": "Using official CDC vaccination data from a comparable period after vaccines became\navailable (October 11, 2021 to January 3, 2022), we confirm a strong negative correlation (r = −0.77, p < 0.001) between\nvaccine polarization and vaccination rates.", "page": 16},
          {"text": "Albrecht D. Vaccination, politics and COVID-19 impacts.", "page": 29}
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
